'''Charxes''' ({{IPA-bl|ˈtʃarɡes}}; {{Literal translation|cultivated fields}}; {{Langx|kp|Ka'aygara|4=canoe forest}}) is a borough of the [[Castejón|Castejón Metropolis]], within the [[Concordia|Concordia conurbation]]. Located in the relatively flat western portion of the metropolis, it is the sixth largest borough of Castejón by area. It is bordered by [[Coruxón]] and [[Llanito]] to the south and southeast. With the exception of it's [[Ciudad Cooperativa, Charxes|waterfront area]] along the [[Alagón (river)|Alagón river]], the borough is almost entirely landlocked.

With a population of 3,253,903 as of the [[2025 Baliscan census|2025 census]], Charxes is the third-most populous borough in the Castejón Metropolis, and is also the sixth-most populous [[cantons of Balisca|third-level]] administrative division in the Baliscan Confederation. Were it an independent city, Charxes would be the sixth most-populous in Balisca. 

Charxes was established in 1635 as one of the [[Alpujarra|original 8 settlements]] of the [[Alpujarra]]. The city developed in the post-independence period as a market and trading city due to its advantageous location on the [[Alagón (river)|Alagón river]]. It remained an independent commune (and previously an authorized village and town within the provisions of the [[Charter of Metropolitan Castejón]]) until October 1902, when, after a long political campaign and public relations battle during the 1890s, [[consolidation of Castejón|Charxes was consolidated]] with other cities, towns, and communes, to form the modern ''Castejón Metropolis,'' surrounding [[Salto Bay]] with six constituent boroughs. The borough continues, however, to maintain a [[Culture of Castejón|distinct culture]]. Approximately 30% of the residents of Charxes are of a nationality other than Baliscan.

It is a popular tourist destination due to its eccentric styles of architecture and large {{W|old town}} that has been preserved from demolition and renovated. Landmarks in Charxes include [[Exposition Park (Castejón)|Exposition Park]]; [[La Portada Stadium|La Portada]] stadium (home to the [[Santiago Juventus]] {{W|Association football|football}} club); the [[Arco dei Caduti|Arch of the Fallen]], and [[Marxuac Plaza]]. Charxes' [[Santiago]] barrio is undergoing rapid [[W:Verticalization|verticalization]] as a result of increasing housing demand.

